vulkan-game
  server.cpp                server application, authoritative world and rules
  client.cpp                client application and input settings
  srv/                      game simulation, scene serialization and authority
  clt/                      client rendering, prediction and weapon presentation
  shd/                      game shaders
  assets/                   game art

Build:
  c build vulkan-server
  c build vulkan-client
The build pins VulkanEngine and VulkanSystems. VulkanNetwork uses Mbed TLS
3.6.7 with DTLS 1.2, mandatory mutual X.509 authentication and forward-secret
ECDHE-AES-GCM cipher suites. All gameplay, movement, weapon and reliable scene
packets use the DTLS socket; there is no plaintext application fallback.
The game owns its scene and authority protocols; network/session is reusable.

Networking (automatic by default):
  c build run vulkan-server
  c build run vulkan-client --host 127.0.0.1 --port 27015
  c build run vulkan-client --host YOUR_PUBLIC_IP --port 27015

  The server binds UDP 0.0.0.0:27015 by default. For another server use its
  public IP or DNS name; forward UDP 27015 on its router and allow inbound UDP
  27015 in the host firewall. The client automatically starts the DTLS
  handshake and uses the same protocol on localhost, LAN and WAN.

  No manual OpenSSL steps, CA distribution or client certificates are required
  by the automatic mode. VulkanNetwork creates a persistent P-256 server key in
  the OS user config folder. It generates a new self-signed X.509 certificate
  from that key at startup. The client pins the server's SubjectPublicKeyInfo
  fingerprint separately for each configured host:port after the first DTLS
  handshake; subsequent connections reject a changed server key.

  SECURITY: automatic TOFU does not authenticate an entirely new server during
  the first connection. An active on-path attacker can impersonate that initial
  server. For strongly authenticated remote servers use a trusted CA or verify
  the logged server fingerprint independently. Do not silently re-pin a changed
  server after reinstalling; restore the original key or explicitly re-enroll
  the endpoint. TOFU authenticates the server after pinning, NOT players:
  client admission and game account security remain separate.

  Existing explicitly provisioned mutual TLS remains supported when ALL
  VULKAN_NET_CA, VULKAN_NET_CERT and VULKAN_NET_KEY are set. In that mode
  VULKAN_NET_SERVER_NAME optionally overrides the expected server identity.
  A partial set of credential environment variables is rejected.

  The server automatically discovers neither clients nor a public address.
  Across the internet, the client must know the host/IP and UDP port.
  Router port forwarding is only effective when the server has a reachable
  public IP (or suitable IPv6 firewall allowance). CGNAT may require a relay,
  VPN or publicly reachable VPS. LAN discovery and NAT traversal are separate
  optional features.

Security and deployment limits:
  Certificates authenticate transport peers, not game accounts or permissions.
  Production deployments need admission controls, certificate revocation,
  rate limiting, incident response, NAT traversal and packet-loss testing.
  The transport is DTLS 1.2, not QUIC/DTLS 1.3. DTLS protects packet
  confidentiality, integrity and replay; reliable ordering-independent bulk
  scene messages are handled separately by network/session. There is no
  guarantee of fixed bandwidth, latency or GPU frame time.

Native compilation, handshake interoperability and platform/runtime regression
testing are required before treating this revision as a release.

Rendering and reusable systems:
  VulkanSystems/render owns general indexed geometry buffers, sampled G-buffer
  attachment images, render target/frame boilerplate and ordered GPU graph
  recording. The client supplies scene snapshots, material/animation payloads,
  GPU draw parameters, game-specific deferred shaders and presentation policy.
  clt/render/passes.* is removed: clt/render/renderer.cpp submits its four
  callbacks through render::sequence. No new GI renderer or global lighting
  policy is embedded in VulkanSystems. The existing game uses GPU culling and
  streamed indexed meshes through render::geometry with nonblocking uploads.

  Emissive RGB is packed through the otherwise unused alpha channels of the
  existing three G-buffer targets, avoiding a fourth target. The light pass
  computes per-pixel view rays using the camera FOV and basis vectors.
  BVH TLAS/BLAS ray queries are an optional VulkanSystems capability and are
  not dispatched by the normal rasterization path; no BVH work is performed
  without a consumer requiring ray/visibility queries.
